- `Cin` (1-bit): Carry-in input
- `g` (4-bit): Input vector of g_i signals
- `p` (4-bit): Input vector of p_i signals

Outputs:
- `c` (4-bit): Output vector of carry bits
- `gout` (1-bit): Output carry generator signal
- `pout` (1-bit): Output propagate generator signal

You are to use two-level logic to design the module, where the first level consists of two 2-bit lookahead carry generators, and the second level is a single 2-bit lookahead carry generator that combines the outputs of the first level.

Use the following modules in your design:
- `lac2`: 2-bit lookahead carry generator
- `lac`: 2-bit lookahead carry generator that combines the outputs of two `lac2` modules

Here is the interface for the `lac2` module:
```
module lac2 (c, gout, pout, Cin, g, p);
  output reg c;
  output gout, pout;
  input Cin;
  input g, p;
endmodule
```

And here is the interface for the `lac` module:
```
module lac (c, gout, pout, Cin, g, p);
  output reg [1:0] c;
  output gout, pout;
  input Cin;
  input [1:0] g, p;
endmodule
```

Your module should instantiate two `lac2` modules to generate the carry bits for the first and second halves of the output vector `c`, and then instantiate a `lac` module to combine the outputs of the two `lac2` modules.

Response:
module lookahead_carry_generator (c, gout, pout, Cin, g, p);
  output reg [3:0] c;
  output reg gout, pout;
  input Cin;
  input [3:0] g, p;

  wire [1:0] cint, gint, pint;

  lac2 leaf0(
     .c(c[1:0]),
     .gout(gint[0]),
     .pout(pint[0]),
     .Cin(Cin),
     .g(g[1:0]),
     .p(p[1:0])
  );

  lac2 leaf1(
     .c(c[3:2]),
     .gout(gint[1]),
     .pout(pint[1]),
     .Cin(gint[0]),
     .g(g[3:2]),
     .p(p[3:2])
  );

  lac root(
     .c(cint),
     .gout(gout),
     .pout(pout),
     .Cin(pint[1]),
     .g(gint),
     .p(pint)
  );

  assign c = {cint[1], cint[0]};
endmodule

module lac2 (c, gout, pout, Cin, g, p);
  output reg c;
  output reg gout, pout;
  input Cin;
  input g, p;

  always @(*) begin
    gout = g[0] & g[1] | g[0] & Cin | g[1] & Cin;
    pout = g[0] | g[1];
    c = pout & Cin | gout;
  end
endmodule

module lac (c, gout, pout, Cin, g, p);
  output reg [1:0] c;
  output reg gout, pout;
  input Cin;
  input [1:0] g, p;

  always @(*) begin
    gout = g[0] & g[1] | g[0] & Cin | g[1] & Cin;
    pout = g[0] | g[1];
    c[1] = pout & Cin | gout;
    c[0] = p[0] & Cin | g[0] & p[1] | g[1] & p[0];
  end
endmodule

