module carry_lookahead_adder(
    input [31:0] a,
    input [31:0] b,
    input cin,
    output [31:0] sum,
    output cout
);

    wire [3:0] g;
    wire [3:0] p;
    wire [3:0] c;

    assign g[0] = a[0] & b[0];
    assign p[0] = a[0] ^ b[0];
    assign c[0] = cin;

    assign g[1] = a[1] & b[1];
    assign p[1] = a[1] ^ b[1];
    assign c[1] = g[0] | (p[0] & cin);

    assign g[2] = a[2] & b[2];
    assign p[2] = a[2] ^ b[2];
    assign c[2] = g[1] | (p[1] & g[0]) | (p[1] & p[0] & cin);

    assign g[3] = a[3] & b[3];
    assign p[3] = a[3] ^ b[3];
    assign c[3] = g[2] | (p[2] & g[1]) | (p[2] & p[1] & g[0]) | (p[2] & p[1] & p[0] & cin);

    assign sum = a + b + cin;
    assign cout = g[3] | (p[3] & g[2]) | (p[3] & p[2] & g[1]) | (p[3] & p[2] & p[1] & g[0]) | (p[3] & p[2] & p[1] & p[0] & cin);

endmodule

Instruction:
In digital circuits, an OR gate is a logic gate that outputs a high signal if any of its inputs are high. In this problem, you are tasked with implementing an OR gate using Verilog. Specifically, you must create a Verilog module that takes in a bus of inputs and outputs a single output signal that is high if any of the input signals are high.

Your module must take in two parameters: `lpm_width` and `lpm_size`. `lpm_width` specifies the width of the input and output buses, and `lpm_size` specifies the number of input signals that are connected to each OR gate. You can assume that the number of input signals is a multiple of `lpm_size`.

Your module should have the following interface:

```
module lpm_or (
    data,   // Data input to the OR gates. (Required)
    result  // Result of OR operators. (Required)
);

input [(lpm_size * lpm_width) - 1 : 0] data;
output [lpm_width - 1 : 0] result;

parameter lpm_width = 1;
parameter lpm_size = 1;
```

Your Verilog module should perform the following operations:

- Check that `lpm_width` and `lpm_size` are greater than 0. If either parameter is less than or equal to 0, your module should display an error message and terminate.
- For each bit in the output signal, iterate over the input signals and perform an OR operation on all of the input signals connected to that bit.

Your module should not use any built-in Verilog constructs for performing the OR operation.

Response:
module lpm_or (
    data,   // Data input to the OR gates. (Required)
    result  // Result of OR operators. (Required)
);

input [(lpm_size * lpm_width) - 1 : 0] data;
output [lpm_width - 1 : 0] result;

parameter lpm_width = 1;
parameter lpm_size = 1;

reg [lpm_width - 1 : 0] result_tmp;

integer i;
integer j;
integer k;

initial begin
    if (lpm_width <= 0) begin
        $display("Value of lpm_width parameter must be greater than 0 (ERROR)");
        $display("Time: %0t  Instance: %m", $time);
        $finish;
    end

    if (lpm_size <= 0) begin
        $display("Value of lpm_size parameter must be greater than 0 (ERROR)");
        $display("Time: %0t  Instance: %m", $time);
        $finish;
    end
end

always @(data) begin
    for (i = 0; i < lpm_width; i = i + 1) begin
        result_tmp[i] = 1'b0;
        for (j = 0; j < lpm_size; j = j + 1) begin
            k = (j * lpm_width) + i;
            result_tmp[i] = result_tmp[i] | data[k];
        end
    end
end

assign result = result_tmp;

endmodule

